[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a quick-reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a search / geocoder box to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map**.  
It’s split into three sections:

| Section | What it is | When you’d pick it | Key pros / cons | Quick-start code |
|---------|------------|--------------------|-----------------|------------------|
| **1️⃣ Official Mapbox Geocoder** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` – a ready-made UI widget that talks to the **Mapbox Geocoding API**. | You already have a Mapbox access token, need the most feature-complete search (autocomplete, fuzzy matching, language bias, proximity, bounding-box, etc.) and are okay with Mapbox’s pricing / usage limits. | ✅ Full-featured, well-documented, works out-of-the-box with Mapbox GL JS v2.<br>❌ Requires a Mapbox token (paid after free tier).<br>❌ Tied to Mapbox’s terms of service. | See the “Official Mapbox Geocoder” snippet below. |
| **2️⃣ Open-source / self-hosted alternatives** | UI widgets that call **open geocoding services** (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, OpenCage, MapTiler Geocoding, etc.). | You want to avoid Mapbox fees, need an open licence, or already run your own geocoder. | ✅ No Mapbox token required; you can self-host for unlimited requests.<br>✅ Many services are free for low-volume use.<br>❌ You must provide your own UI (or use a community widget) and handle rate-limits / attribution. | See the “Open-source UI widgets” snippets below. |
| **3️⃣ DIY UI + direct API calls** | Build a tiny `<input>` + fetch-/axios-based request to any HTTP geocoder you like. | You need a custom look-and-feel, want to combine several services, or only need a very simple “type-ahead → fly-to” flow. | ✅ Full control over UI and request logic.<br>❌ More code to write and maintain.<br>❌ You still need to respect the service’s usage policy. | See the “Custom minimal geocoder” snippet below. |

---

## 1️⃣ Official Mapbox Geocoder (Mapbox-provided UI)

### When to use it
* You already have a **Mapbox access token** (free tier or paid plan).  
* You need **autocomplete**, **reverse-geocoding**, **language / proximity bias**, **bounding-box limits**, **category filters**, etc.  
* You want the **quickest possible integration** with minimal code.

### Install

```bash
# npm / yarn
npm install @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder mapbox-gl
# or, if you’re using a CDN, just pull the script / CSS from unpkg
```

### Basic usage (ES-modules)

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

// 1️⃣ Set your token (required for the geocoder *and* the map)
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

// 2️⃣ Create the map (v2 syntax)
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12
});

// 3️⃣ Create the geocoder widget
const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken, // same token as the map
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,                // required for the built-in UI
  placeholder: 'Search places …',
  // optional tweaks:
  //   proximity: { longitude: -122.447303, latitude: 37.753574 },
  //   bbox: [-123, 36, -121, 38],
  //   language: 'en',
  //   countries: 'us,ca',
  //   limit: 5,
});

// 4️⃣ Add the widget to the map UI (or any DOM node you like)
map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');
```

### What you get “for free”

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Autocomplete** | Calls `https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/<query>.json` on each keystroke. |
| **Result list** | Shows a dropdown with place name, address, and an optional icon. |
| **Fly-to on select** | The widget automatically calls `map.flyTo({center, zoom})`. |
| **Reverse geocode** | `geocoder.on('result', e => …)` gives you `e.result` (full GeoJSON feature). |
| **Biasing** | `proximity`, `bbox`, `countries`, `language`, `types`, `autocomplete` flags. |
| **Styling** | The widget uses a CSS class `mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder`. Override it in your own stylesheet if you need a custom look. |

### Pricing & limits (as of 2026-11)

| Tier | Free quota | Cost beyond free |
|------|------------|------------------|
| **Free** | 100 000 forward geocode requests / month, 100 000 reverse per month. | N/A (subject to rate-limit of 600 req/min). |
| **Pay-as-you-go** | $0.50 per 1 000 forward requests, $0.75 per 1 000 reverse. | No hard caps, but you must stay within your account’s usage limits. |

> **Tip:** If you only need a few hundred look-ups per month, the free tier is usually enough. For production-scale apps, consider a **self-hosted Nominatim** or **MapTiler Geocoding** (see section 2).

---

## 2️⃣ Open-source / self-hosted alternatives

Below are the most common open geocoders you can pair with a lightweight UI widget. All of them return **GeoJSON-compatible** results (or can be transformed easily) and can be used with Mapbox GL JS v2 exactly the same way as the Mapbox geocoder—just replace the request URL.

| Service | URL pattern (GET) | License / hosting | Typical free quota | Example UI widget |
|---------|-------------------|-------------------|--------------------|-------------------|
| **Nominatim (OpenStreetMap)** | `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&q={query}&addressdetails=1&limit=5` | Public instance (CC-BY-SA) – **not for production**; self-hosted (GPL-2.0) | 1 req/s per IP (public) | `leaflet-control-geocoder` (works with any map) or a tiny custom widget |
| **Photon (Komoot)** | `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q={query}&limit=5` | Public instance (Apache-2.0) – **rate-limited**; self-hostable (Apache-2.0) | 5 req/s per IP (public) | `@geocoder-io/photon-geocoder` (community wrapper) |
| **Pelias** | `https://pelias.example.com/v1/search?text={query}&size=5` | Open-source (MIT) – you must run your own instance (or use a hosted provider) | Unlimited on self-host | No official UI – use any generic autocomplete component |
| **OpenCage** | `https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?q={query}&key=YOUR_KEY&limit=5` | Commercial SaaS (CC-BY-4.0 for free tier) | 2 500 req/day free | `@opencage/geocoder` (community wrapper) |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY&limit=5` | Commercial SaaS (BSD-3) – free tier 100 k req/mo | 100 k req/mo free | `@maptiler/geocoder` (official widget) |
| **Algolia Places** (deprecated, still works) | `https://places-dsn.algolia.net/1/places/query` (POST) | MIT – **service shutting down** (use only for legacy) | N/A | `places.js` (old UI) |

### 2A. Using a ready-made UI widget that works with any geocoder

The **`@mapbox/search-js`** package (originally built for Mapbox) is **geocoder-agnostic** – you give it a `fetch` function that points at any endpoint. It ships a clean, minimal UI that you can style.

```bash
npm install @mapbox/search-js
```

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import { SearchBox } from '@mapbox/search-js';
import '@mapbox/search-js/dist/search-js.css';

// 1️⃣ Create the map (same as before)
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Helper that talks to Nominatim (you can swap this for Photon, Pelias, etc.)
async function nominatimFetch(query) {
  const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search');
  url.searchParams.set('format', 'json');
  url.searchParams.set('addressdetails', '1');
  url.searchParams.set('limit', '5');
  url.searchParams.set('q', query);
  const resp = await fetch(url);
  const data = await resp.json();
  // Convert Nominatim’s format to the GeoJSON FeatureCollection that SearchBox expects
  return {
    features: data.map(p => ({
      type: 'Feature',
      geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [parseFloat(p.lon), parseFloat(p.lat)] },
      properties: {
        // The UI shows `text` and `place_name`
        text: p.display_name,
        place_name: p.display_name,
        // Keep the raw Nominatim payload if you need it later
        raw: p
      }
    }))
  };
}

// 3️⃣ Create the SearchBox UI
const searchBox = new SearchBox({
  placeholder: 'Search OSM …',
  // The widget calls this function on each keystroke
  onSearch: nominatimFetch,
  // When a result is chosen, fly the map there
  onSelect: (result) => {
    const [lng, lat] = result.geometry.coordinates;
    map.flyTo({ center: [lng, lat], zoom: 14 });
  }
});

// 4️⃣ Insert the UI into the page (you can also add it as a map control)
document.getElementById('geocoder-container').appendChild(searchBox.element);
```

**Why use `@mapbox/search-js`?**  
* It’s a **tiny, framework-agnostic** component (no React/Vue dependency).  
* It works with **any HTTP geocoder** as long as you return a GeoJSON `FeatureCollection`.  
* You keep the same look-and-feel as the Mapbox Geocoder (you can style the CSS to match your app).

### 2B. Using the community-maintained **`maplibre-gl-geocoder`** (works with Mapbox GL JS v2)

`maplibre-gl-geocoder` is a fork of the official Mapbox widget that removes the hard dependency on a Mapbox token. It ships with a **plug-in architecture** that lets you register any “geocoder service”.

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

```js
